The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (unlikely(!test_bit(SDF_JOURNAL_LIVE, &sdp->sd_flags))) {
    lockref_mark_dead(&qd->qd_lockref);
    spin_unlock(&qd->qd_lockref.lock);
    gfs2_qd_dispose(qd);
    return;
}
Reason:
When the filesystem is shutting down (SDF_JOURNAL_LIVE not set), `qd_put` directly calls `gfs2_qd_dispose(qd)` to free the quota data object. It does **not** remove the object from the global LRU list (`gfs2_qd_lru`) before freeing. This leaves a stale pointer on the LRU list; later, the shrinker (`gfs2_qd_shrink_scan`) traverses the LRU and accesses the already-freed object, causing slab-use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (unlikely(!test_bit(SDF_JOURNAL_LIVE, &sdp->sd_flags))) {
    lockref_mark_dead(&qd->qd_lockref);
    spin_unlock(&qd->qd_lockref.lock);
    list_lru_del_obj(&gfs2_qd_lru, &qd->qd_lru);
    gfs2_qd_dispose(qd);
    return;
}
Reason:
The added line `list_lru_del_obj(&gfs2_qd_lru, &qd->qd_lru);` removes the quota data object from the LRU list before it is freed by `gfs2_qd_dispose(qd)`. This ensures that the shrinker will not find a dangling reference to the freed object, thus preventing the use-after-free.

